      "summary": "THE COVERING OBLIGATION \u2014 what makes the atlas carve legitimate rather than lossy\n(spec-dd-r ADDENDUM 3 \u00a75). With p1-aut, this closes the certificate lane's geometric gate.\n\nSTATEMENT. For a relative divisor whose support is finite over Spec R, and any s in Spec R: the\nfibre D_s is finite, so pi(D_s) is a finite subset of P^1 over kappa(s); if k is INFINITE then\nP^1(k) is infinite, so two distinct c_0, c_1 in P^1(k) avoid pi(D_s). relCurve C R -> Spec R is\nproper (instIsProperRelCurveHom, SupportTube.lean:194) hence closed, so the image of\nsupportLocus cap pi^-1({c_0,c_1}) is closed and misses s. Over the open complement the divisor is\nconfined to pi^-1(P^1 \\ {c_0,c_1}) = V_0 inf V_1 for the gamma-twist carrying {c_0,c_1} to\n{0,infinity}.\n\nPAYOFF, ALREADY LANDED: chartPairCoverData / ofChartPair and\nforall_finite_colength_of_pieces_eq_chart (Picard/DivSchemeCertZarChartPair.lean) then give\nclause (c1) FREE \u2014 no fibre, no tube, no packet idempotent. The base-open spread it needs is\nconfine-open's exists_opens_supportLocus_subset_chartInter.\n\nTWO HYPOTHESES TO WATCH, both real risks, neither to be assumed silently:\n (i) k INFINITE. Challenge.lean states the Jacobian over an arbitrary field. A finite-field route\n     needs |P^1(k)| > deg D, i.e. a base change to k(T) or a degree-d point; schedule it, do not\n     assume it.\n (ii) FINITENESS OF THE SUPPORT OVER THE BASE must come from the seed's own degree data, NOT from\n     the certificate \u2014 otherwise the argument is circular. This is the one genuinely load-bearing\n     input of this leaf.\n",
